raw_text:
@sarthak

Ek Delhi ki  journalist ki khabar aapne nahi batai, shayad wo news aapki feed me nahi aai hogi.

If possible please make a separate video.

24:55 , Why did you say that quota was involved? Have you checked her degree or the institute that awarded it? If she completed a nursing course there, there should have been a proper training and practical system in place. If she passed the course without gaining adequate practical experience, then that is a failure of the college’s education and training system—not reservation.

Also, it’s not as if only students from one caste or category study in a college. Students from General and other categories study there as well. If someone from any category reaches a position without proper training or competence, they could make the same mistakes. So blaming reservation for the actions of one individual doesn’t make sense.

@Sundaysarthak

@SarthakGoswami Subscribed to your channel in 2022 and never regretted it. Your Sunday shows genuinely changed how I think about journalism - beyond headlines, binaries, and noise.

Loved Bihar for Beginners, and then the Bengal series. Thank you for keeping space for context, nuance, and broader conversations.

One question I’ve always wondered: When journalism starts helping people understand each other instead of just informing them, does it still remain journalism - or does it become something bigger?

@SundaySarthak Bhaiyya I am not really aware about what happens throughout the country when it comes to casteism because I am raised in a family jaha my parents say caste doesn't matter the standard of education matters. But having lived across India (changed 7 schools is 7 different locations btw), I have seen some acquaintances of mine follow the "uske ghar ka khana nahi khaungi" or "hum xyz caste ke hai mai kaise fail ho sakta hoon exam mein" mindset.

What I sincerely believe is that the reservation system needs to be modified a little. Instead of nationwide Caste based reservation, it should be restricted to rural areas and under-developed villages where caste-based discrimination is still very much prevalent. Whereas for urban areas where family income matters more than the caste of an individual, reservation should be based on family income. 

(Anybody willing to hold a healthy discussion is welcome but kindly refrain from rude language, using abusive words & Maa Behen ki gaaliyan in the responses. Remember Profanity is the trait of a weak personality.)

